import java.util.TreeMap;

public class MyCalendarThree {
    TreeMap<Integer, Integer> calendar;

    public MyCalendarThree() {
        calendar = new TreeMap<>();
    }

    public int book(int start, int end) {
        calendar.put(start, calendar.getOrDefault(start, 0) + 1); // increment the counter at start time of the event
        calendar.put(end, calendar.getOrDefault(end, 0) - 1);  // decrement the counter at end time of the event

        int ongoing = 0, max = 0;
        for (int v : calendar.values()) {
            ongoing += v;
            max = Math.max(max, ongoing);
        }

        return max;
    }
}
```
Explanation:
In the constructor we initialize the TreeMap. We add 1 at the start time of an event and subtract 1 at end time of an event. For each added event we keep a running sum ("ongoing") and keep track of the maximum of this sum. This will be the maximum k-booking. We use a TreeMap so that the start and end times are automatically sorted.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n log n) where n is the number of events booked, because TreeMap operations take log n. The space complexity is O(n) because we store each start and end time in the TreeMap.

